the Long Island horticultural research  and Extension Center is a campus of  Cornell University we've been here in  Riverhead New York for 98 years and our  purpose here is to help the agricultural  industry  succeed there is a lot of need for  up-to-date research and assistance with  growing plants the impatiens is  primarily of interest to the greenhouse  growers on Long Island as well as some  of the nurseries the commercial the  retail establishments that sell plants  right here we've been breeding in  patients plants trying to develop  resistance to the disease called  impatience downy mildew and there's  always problems when you do breeding and  one of the one of the issues that we  found out with this plant is they have  what we call explosive seed pods so that  means when the seed pods are ripe thanks  but and of course if you're trying to  harvest seed that's a problem because  then they're all over the greenhouse  floor so we use we use a technique  called plant tissue culture to assist  our breeding program and essentially  plant tissue culture is a way to grow  plants in a sterile environment on a  nutrient medium under controlled  environment so we will remove the pods  that we've pollinated on these plants  about 14 to 16 days after we've  pollinated them and we take those pods  to our laboratory they're sterilized on  the outside and then they're opened  aseptic ly to expose the seeds and those  seeds that are placed in tissue culture  to germinate the nice thing about that  process is is that once they germinate  in tissue culture they're always going  to be there as a stock plan for us  they're ours gonna be available we can  take cuttings of those plans to bring  them out here in the greenhouse if we  find out that we have a successful plant  a successful hybrid we have the mother  plant in tissue culture free of diseases  free of insects and we can then rapidly  propagate that plant to increase the  numbers quickly  and these are the results of some of  that breeding research so all these  plants that you see here are resistant  to the disease we've done field trials  for them for the last two years but our  next step then is to produce a plant  that has a nice looking flower and a  nice symmetrical growth habit so we are  continuing to hybridize these plants and  will continue to develop and until we  get a nice seed profit
 

